WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.754
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.754 --> 00:00:05.200
I help some urban developers
get their sensors straight.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.362
Will we be able to
find parking in time?

00:00:07.362 --> 00:00:08.320
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.590
Like most IoT
companies, Urban IoT

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.270
makes their money
deploying hordes

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.670
of sensors in an urban area
that monitor everything

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.699
from noise, temperature, and
humidity, which of course means

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:23.740
that the majority of their
data that they collect

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.620
from those devices consists
of timestamps and simple

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.630
floating point numbers,
the volume of which

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.480
quickly started showing
a problem with Datastore

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:32.049
performance.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:35.710
See, Cloud Datastore is built
on top of Bigtable, and thus,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.230
is subject to its
performance characteristics.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.580
Bigtable scales by sharding rows
into separate tablets, which

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.070
are basically a contiguous
unit of storage.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.820
And when a tablet experiences
a high write rate,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.330
the tablet will split, allowing
it to scale even further.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.760
The important point
here is this-- rows

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.070
in the tablet are
ordered such that it

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.550
helps distribute the workload
amongst multiple tablets.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.510
They do this by sorting the rows
lexicographically by their key.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.640
And as long as the keys
are randomly distributed,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.960
this sorting works pretty fine.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.495
However, if your keys are
lexicographically close,

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:13.120
then your performance
starts to suffer,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.850
as your operations can be
isolated into a small update

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.930
window, which, lo and behold,
was exactly what Urban IoT was

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:20.740
doing.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.020
Turns out their
federation of IoT devices

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.970
were pushing sensor
readings into Datastore

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.700
and using the timestamp
as the key for the entity

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.200
so they could easily fetch
and display the results later.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.300
Timestamps, of course, are a
monotonically increasing value,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.870
which is being used as an
index property, which led right

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.420
into the hop spotting scenario
that we were worried about.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.740
Now here's the really
interesting part.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.180
This behavior was really
hard to repo on our side.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.810
I mean, we set up a 250 QPS
load test creating entities

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.720
with keys that are timestamps
over a 20-minute window

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:54.730
and didn't see anything.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.060
Datastore did an amazing job at
absorbing that level of work,

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.650
no problems whatsoever.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.360
In order to actually
see this behavior,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.480
we had to jack the QPS up
to 750 and let the test

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.776
run for over an hour.

00:02:05.776 --> 00:02:07.900
You can see that over time
it takes a little longer

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.639
to handle the same operations.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:13.120
For Urban IoT, the least
invasive fix for this problem

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.420
was to append a random prefix
to their timestamps, which

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.872
caused some headaches in
their dashboard for fetching,

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.830
but also allowed their
performance to land back

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.900
in the non-hot-spotted area.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.820
Now I just want to
highlight something again.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.201
This is not a standard,
run-of-the-mill performance

00:02:27.201 --> 00:02:27.700
issue.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.030
It took a couple of days of
really hammering on Datastore

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.820
to get our tests to behave the
way that Urban IoT was seeing

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.540
in the wild.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.130
And my guess is
that unless your use

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.500
case is very similar
to theirs, chances

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.940
are you won't run into it.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:43.136
So if you'd like ideas on where
to spend your time instead,

00:02:43.136 --> 00:02:45.760
make sure you check out the rest
of the Cloud Performance Atlas

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.509
content.

00:02:46.509 --> 00:02:48.050
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.091
every millisecond counts.

00:02:49.091 --> 00:02:52.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

